```
@id("close-workorder-main-with-human-approval")
permit (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"close_workorder", resource)
when { context.input.site_id == "MAIN" }
when temporal {
  formerly within 1h Drupe::Action::"get_workorder"::response{
    eventResource: resource,
    input.wonum: context.input.wonum,
    input.site_id: context.input.site_id,
    output.approval_outcome: "GRANTED",
    output.approver_type: "HUMAN"
  }
};

// Read-only lookups. get_workorder must be permitted, or its response is never recorded
// and the close policy above could never hold.
@id("allow-read-only-lookups")
permit (principal, action in [
  Drupe::Action::"get_workorder",
  Drupe::Action::"authority_get_approval",
  Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_tasks",
  Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_costs",
  Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_actuals_vs_planned",
  Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_kpis",
  Drupe::Action::"get_schedule_calendar",
  Drupe::Action::"get_my_assigned_workorders",
  Drupe::Action::"get_failure_codes",
  Drupe::Action::"list_workorders"
], resource);
```

`approve_workorder` has no permit, so it is denied by default and the agent cannot approve anything itself. The same goes for `generate_work_order`, `update_workorder`, `assign_technician` and `cancel_workorder`, which the intent doesn't allow.

I used `get_workorder` as the evidence of approval because its output carries `approval_outcome` (`GRANTED`, `REJECTED` or `PENDING`) and `approver_type` (`HUMAN`, `AGENT` or `SYSTEM`). The close policy only accepts a `GRANTED` outcome from a `HUMAN` approver. The lookup must be a completed `::response` in this session within the last hour. It must also match the `wonum` and `site_id` of the close request, and the close itself must be at `MAIN`.

I matched those enum fields with plain string literals, as the reference describes. If your validator insists on entity-typed values for them, they would need to be written as `Drupe::get_workorder_Output_approval_outcome::"GRANTED"` and its `approver_type` counterpart.

I did not use `authority_get_approval` as the evidence. Its `decision` and `approver_kind` outputs are free strings with no declared values, so I would have had to guess what to match. It is still permitted as a read-only lookup.
